            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for the ceremony, then the German for to open officially."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: gratulieren",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gratulieren — \"to congratulate\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Alle gratulierten ihr zum Geburtstag. Everyone congratulated her on her birthday."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "With the dative and zu: Ich gratuliere dir zum Abitur."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gratulieren is a learned borrowing from Latin grātulārī \"to wish joy\", built on grātus \"pleasing, thankful\" (Lewis & Short); EtymDB lists no parent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
